course: dictionary
lesson: 6066
title: Meridians: the lines that run pole to pole
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A meridian is an imaginary line on a globe or a map. It runs from the North Pole to the South Pole, like a line drawn from the top of the world to the bottom. «Lines of longitude, called meridians, run from pole to pole and all meet at the poles.»
explain: Map makers use meridians as a grid, a bit like the lines on squared paper that help you find a square. Longitude tells us how far east or west a place is, and it is measured from a meridian.
explain: Context gives the clue. «Longitude measures how far east or west a place is from the prime meridian.» The words far east or west tell you the meridian is a line that we measure from. Swap in the word line and the sentence still makes sense, so you have worked it out.
fact: A meridian is an imaginary line from the North Pole to the South Pole.
fact: Lines of longitude are meridians.
fact: The Prime Meridian is the meridian at 0 degrees longitude, and it runs through Greenwich in London.
fact: Time zones are counted from meridians. In 1884 countries chose Greenwich as 0 degrees longitude.
fact: Meridian has other meanings: the highest point of the sun at midday (old, poetic), and in Chinese medicine, meridians are paths of energy in the body, a belief that science has not proved.
exercise: «Longitude measures how far east or west a place is from the prime meridian.» What is a meridian? | A kind of cloud that hangs over mountains | A river that flows into the sea | A type of rock found on beaches | *An imaginary line from the North Pole to the South Pole
exercise: Where does the Prime Meridian run? | Through the middle of the Pacific Ocean | *Through Greenwich in London | Along the equator | In Paris, France
exercise: Which lines on a map are meridians? | *Lines of longitude | Lines of latitude, like the equator | The Tropic of Cancer | The Arctic Circle
test: Why are meridians useful on a map? | They show where the rain falls every day | They show how tall the mountains are | *They help us find how far east or west a place is | They show the colour of the sea
test: Trains in Britain used London time from the 1840s. Why did countries need a line at 0 degrees? | To mark where the snow lies all year | To show where the sun never sets | So that ships could sail faster | *So that they could agree on time zones
exam: Which meaning of meridian is the old, poetic one? | Any line on a map that goes north to south | A town in Greenwich | *The highest point of the sun at midday | A path of energy in the body
